// SPEC 5.2 / 5.2.1 / 5.2.1.1 -- what a `region` actually serves, asserted at run
// time.
//
// Nothing in this suite has ever checked that an arena allocates anything. The
// manifest said `region:` and the tests read values, and both were satisfied by
// a region that bump-allocated nothing at all -- which is how `g2_region.psm`
// came to be quoted as the language's tuning story for three sessions while
// serving 0 of 10 201 215 allocations and running 1.67x slower.
//
// `arena_objects()` is the only way to tell an arena pointer from a malloc'd one:
// the values are identical. Sampled as a *delta* around each region rather than
// as a total, because std/io's own string sites are auto-arena-placed and a total
// would couple this fixture to how many times it prints. `ir_alloc_region`
// deliberately bypasses `--verify`'s accounting -- an arena releases in bulk and
// the ledger has nothing to pair a release with -- so the ledger cannot see any
// of this either. This counter is the only witness there is.
//
// **This fixture changed on 2026-08-16, deliberately, and the old version is the
// second half of the record.** It had `serves_nothing`: the same allocation moved
// one function down read 0, because SPEC 5.2.1's arena is lexical. Its header
// said "when call-site placement lands, `serves_nothing` is the test that
// changes". It landed, and it does -- `serves_from_a_callee` is that function
// with the assertion inverted. What replaces it as the pin is the pair below it:
// call-site placement is *conditional*, and a fixture that only checked the case
// that now works would pass on a compiler that bracketed unconditionally, which
// is a use-after-free rather than a lost optimisation.
//
// Four functions, and each one is the discriminator for the next:
//
//   serves                       50 -- a lexical region, SPEC 5.2
//   serves_from_a_callee         50 -- the same allocation one function down,
//                                      reached by bracketing the call (5.2.1.1)
//   outlives_serves_nothing       0 -- obligation 3 alone: identical shape, one
//                                      call site, every other obligation clear,
//                                      and the binding declared *above* the
//                                      region instead of inside it
//   shared_body_in_one_region    51 -- regime (a) generalised: two call sites,
//                                      **both inside the same region**, so the
//                                      body only ever runs with that arena
//                                      innermost and serves exactly one regime
//   shared_body_serves_nothing    0 -- regime (a) as a limit: one of the two
//                                      call sites is **outside** every region,
//                                      so the same body would have to allocate
//                                      from an arena on one path and the heap on
//                                      another
//
// Read the first two together and the last three together. Either group alone
// passes for the wrong reason: the positives alone pass on a compiler that
// brackets everything, and the negatives alone pass on one that brackets
// nothing -- which is exactly the compiler this fixture was written against.
//
// **The fourth and fifth were one function until 2026-08-28**, asserting that
// two call sites blocked bracketing whatever else held. That was regime (a) as
// SPEC 5.2.1.1 first stated it -- "exactly one call site" -- and it is a
// sufficient condition for "one body, one placement regime" rather than a
// necessary one. Two calls that are both inside one region give the same
// guarantee, which is the shape g6's tick loop has: `plan_orders` once per
// squad, from the same loop body. The fourth function is that case, and the
// fifth is what still has to be refused.

import std.io
extern fn print(s: String)
extern fn println(s: String)
extern fn arena_objects() -> Int

struct Cmd {
    id: Int,
    weight: Int
}

fn fail(what: String) -> Int {
    print("FAIL ")
    println(what)
    return 1
}

// The allocation is bound to a name declared in the region's own scope, so its
// escape rises above the allocating block and the T0 clause declines -- T1, in a
// region it cannot outlive, held by no container. Every gate passes.
//
// 50 and not 51: the initialiser `Cmd { id: 0, weight: 0 }` is declared and used
// in one scope, so it is T0 and lives in the frame. Only the 50 assignments in
// the loop body outlive their own block.
fn serves() -> Int {
    let before = arena_objects()
    region work {
        let mut keep = Cmd { id: 0, weight: 0 }
        let mut i = 0
        while (i < 50) {
            keep = Cmd { id: i, weight: i * 2 }
            if (keep.id != i) { return fail("serves value") }
            i = i + 1
        }
    }
    let served = arena_objects() - before
    if (served != 50) {
        print("  served ")
        println(served)
        return fail("serves: expected 50 allocations from the arena")
    }
    return 0
}

// SPEC 5.2.1.1. The identical allocation, one function further down. `make` has
// no region and cannot have one -- an arena is a lexical scope and this is not
// the function the scope is in. What reaches it is the *call*: `make` clears the
// obligations, it has exactly one call site, that site is inside `callee_work`,
// and the value it returns is bound at a scope the region contains. So the
// bracket is legal and the allocation comes from the caller's arena.
//
// No new codegen mechanism does this. `region` already pushes an arena on entry
// and pops it at every exit, so while `make` runs, the caller's arena is the top
// of the dynamic stack; all that changed is that the analysis now says this site
// belongs to it, after which ir_alloc_region -- which has existed since Level 3
// -- routes it there.
fn make(i: Int) -> Cmd {
    return Cmd { id: i, weight: i * 2 }
}

fn serves_from_a_callee() -> Int {
    let before = arena_objects()
    region callee_work {
        let mut keep = Cmd { id: 0, weight: 0 }
        let mut i = 0
        while (i < 50) {
            keep = make(i)
            if (keep.id != i) { return fail("serves_from_a_callee value") }
            i = i + 1
        }
    }
    let served = arena_objects() - before
    if (served != 50) {
        print("  served ")
        println(served)
        return fail("serves_from_a_callee: the bracketed call did not reach the arena")
    }
    return 0
}

// Obligation 3, isolated. `make_out` is byte-for-byte `make`; the region is the
// same shape; the call site is the only one and it is inside the region. The one
// difference is where `keep` is declared -- one line up, in the function's own
// scope rather than the region's -- so the value the call returns is still live
// after the arena is gone.
//
// This is the obligation E cannot express, and the reason it needs its own
// fixture. AIF_CON_LIVE_IN sets E = Caller for every site whose function is not
// the binding function, by construction: a scope id in `make_out` does not order
// against one in this function. So `make_out`'s site has exactly the same E in
// both cases and the answer has to come from the caller-side binding instead.
// A test that read E would pass here and be wrong.
fn make_out(i: Int) -> Cmd {
    return Cmd { id: i, weight: i * 2 }
}

fn outlives_serves_nothing() -> Int {
    let before = arena_objects()
    let mut keep = Cmd { id: 0, weight: 0 }
    region outlived_work {
        let mut i = 0
        while (i < 50) {
            keep = make_out(i)
            if (keep.id != i) { return fail("outlives value") }
            i = i + 1
        }
    }
    if (keep.id != 49) { return fail("outlives: the value did not survive the region") }
    let served = arena_objects() - before
    if (served != 0) {
        print("  served ")
        println(served)
        return fail("outlives: a value that outlives the region came from its arena")
    }
    return 0
}

// Regime (a) generalised, isolated. Two call sites, **both inside the same
// region**, so every caller binding is at or below `r`, obligation 3 holds for
// both, and the body is only ever entered with `shared_work`'s arena innermost.
// One body, one regime -- which is what SPEC 5.2.1.1's table actually asks for.
// Nothing separates this from `serves_from_a_callee` except the call count, and
// the call count is no longer the answer.
//
// 51 and not 50, for the reason `serves` is 50: the loop contributes its 50 and
// the extra call contributes one. `keep`'s initialiser is T0 and stays in the
// frame.
fn make_shared(i: Int) -> Cmd {
    return Cmd { id: i, weight: i * 2 }
}

fn shared_body_in_one_region() -> Int {
    let before = arena_objects()
    region shared_work {
        let mut keep = Cmd { id: 0, weight: 0 }
        let mut i = 0
        while (i < 50) {
            keep = make_shared(i)
            if (keep.id != i) { return fail("shared_body value") }
            i = i + 1
        }
        let extra = make_shared(99)
        if (extra.id != 99) { return fail("shared_body extra value") }
    }
    let served = arena_objects() - before
    if (served != 51) {
        print("  served ")
        println(served)
        return fail("shared_body_in_one_region: two calls in one region is one regime")
    }
    return 0
}

// And regime (a) as a limit, which is the half that still has to refuse.
//
// `make_outside` is byte-for-byte `make_shared`, the region is the same shape,
// and the only difference is one call **outside** every region. That body would
// then have to allocate from an arena on 50 paths and from the heap on one --
// two regimes, and the one without an arena allocates a value nothing frees,
// because a site compiled for a region is off every drop list.
//
// **The discarded result is what isolates it.** No binding holds the value from
// the outside call, so obligation 3 is untouched and this fixture fails on the
// regime clause alone. Bind it instead -- `let x = make_outside(99)` above the
// region -- and obligation 3 rejects too, because the points-to graph is
// context-insensitive and that binding then holds the loop's sites as well; the
// fixture would pass whichever of the two clauses was deleted, which is the
// defect this suite produces most.
fn make_outside(i: Int) -> Cmd {
    return Cmd { id: i, weight: i * 2 }
}

fn shared_body_serves_nothing() -> Int {
    let before = arena_objects()
    make_outside(99)
    region outside_work {
        let mut keep = Cmd { id: 0, weight: 0 }
        let mut i = 0
        while (i < 50) {
            keep = make_outside(i)
            if (keep.id != i) { return fail("shared_body outside value") }
            i = i + 1
        }
    }
    let served = arena_objects() - before
    if (served != 0) {
        print("  served ")
        println(served)
        return fail("shared_body: a call site outside the region was bracketed anyway")
    }
    return 0
}

fn main() -> Int {
    let mut bad = 0
    bad = bad + serves()
    bad = bad + serves_from_a_callee()
    bad = bad + outlives_serves_nothing()
    bad = bad + shared_body_in_one_region()
    bad = bad + shared_body_serves_nothing()
    if (bad != 0) { return 1 }
    println("test_58 ok")
    return 0
}
